9	**Behaviour.**
10	
11	- A word is a maximal run of Unicode letters or digits (`\p{L}`, `\p{N}`), in any script.
12	- A single straight (`'`) or typographic (`’`) apostrophe, or a single hyphen, joins two runs into one word, but only when a letter or digit sits on both sides. `don't`, `keeper’s` and `well-known` are one word each. `well-` and `dogs'` are the words `well` and `dogs`, with the stray joiner dropped.
13	- Everything else separates words: whitespace, sentence punctuation, em and en dashes, slashes, underscores, and the point in a decimal number or domain name. `3.14` is two words (`3` and `14`) and `example.com` is two (`example` and `com`).
14	- A string with no letters or digits has zero words, however long it is.
15	- A non-string argument throws `TypeError` with a message starting `wordCount:`.
